  "metadata": {
    "title": "Specify, Don't Simulate: A Fail-Closed Four-Gate Verification Pipeline for Verifiable GGUF Model Artifacts in the Arkhe OS Trust Layer",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22779781",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-16",
    "description": "<p>Open-weight machine-learning artifacts circulate through distribution chains characterized by opaque provenance,<br>executable metadata, and trust delegated to promises. This article consolidates and extends arkhe.gguf, the<br>model-verification component of the Arkhe OS trust layer, tracking its evolution from a delegation package<br>(schema v1.2) to a complete project structure (schema v1.3). The framework's methodological axiom &mdash; what<br>does not exist is not simulated; it is specified &mdash; separates generated artifacts (a byte-exact 24-byte GGUF v3<br>header fixture whose SHA-256 digest we compute by deterministic execution), delegated execution steps, and<br>explicitly non-generable items. The technical contribution is a fail-closed, four-gate verification pipeline: Gate 0<br>sanitizes GGUF metadata against two verified parser vulnerabilities (CVE-2026-65315, Ollama;<br>CVE-2025-53630, llama.cpp); Gate 1 verifies dual SHA-256/BLAKE3 digests; Gate 2 verifies keyless Sigstore<br>signatures over an in-toto Statement v1 manifest; Gate 3 verifies RFC 6962 inclusion and consistency proofs plus<br>a witness quorum of at least two checkpoint cosignatures. Schema v1.3 adds a source block for pre-extension<br>hashes, an external-manifest hash location, CVE attestation fields, and Safenet-enforced revocation. We further<br>document an honest-delegation methodology, a thirteen-step provenance checklist, tooling errata discovered<br>during verification (rekor-cli flag mismatches), and negative tests that make fail-closed behavior an executable<br>specification. We conclude that attestation constitutes an epistemically honest floor &mdash; signed is not safe, identity<br>is not behavior &mdash; and outline the path from PROMISE to VERIFIED within a complete digital-sovereignty stack.</p>",
